WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:07.650
Florida gets more lightning than any other state, and from June to September a thunderstorm is nearly a daily event.

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.175
This lesson is about the weather that can kill you in an afternoon.

3
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:18.350
Summer storms usually build over the land in the early afternoon, then drift toward the coast.

4
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.600
So the morning is yours.

5
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.400
The afternoon belongs to the storm.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:27.200
If you can hear thunder, you’re close enough to be struck.

7
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.150
Head in early.

8
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.800
The wind arrives before the rain.

9
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.375
Gusts flowing out of a storm can hit at gale force, well ahead of it.

10
00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:42.800
If you’re caught out, get everyone low, away from metal, the rods and the console, and out of the water.

11
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.800
And waterspouts.

12
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.275
They’re common in the Keys and along both coasts.

13
00:00:48.525 --> 00:00:51.200
Most are the weaker fair-weather kind.

14
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.025
But how should you treat every one of them?

15
00:00:55.025 --> 00:00:56.750
As dangerous.

16
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.875
Move away at right angles to its path.
